1
00:00:00,050 --> 00:00:01,887
So let's be honest about the ledger.

2
00:00:01,887 --> 00:00:05,087
What a quantum computer will probably do, if it works.

3
00:00:05,087 --> 00:00:06,887
It will simulate molecules.

4
00:00:06,887 --> 00:00:15,750
Not approximately, the way classical computers do, but natively, because a molecule is a quantum system, and the machine speaks its language.

5
00:00:15,750 --> 00:00:19,812
That could mean better catalysts, better batteries, better fertiliser.

6
00:00:19,812 --> 00:00:28,862
Nitrogen fixation alone consumes about one to two percent of the world's energy, and a quantum computer might find a catalyst that does it at room temperature.

7
00:00:28,862 --> 00:00:32,575
It will break some encryption, and it will help build better encryption.

8
00:00:32,575 --> 00:00:33,362
Both.

9
00:00:33,362 --> 00:00:34,950
That is not a contradiction.

10
00:00:34,950 --> 00:00:38,000
It is what happens when you change the rules of computation.

11
00:00:38,000 --> 00:00:40,337
It will probably not make your phone faster.

12
00:00:40,337 --> 00:00:42,237
It will not run video games.

13
00:00:42,237 --> 00:00:44,025
It will not replace your laptop.

14
00:00:44,025 --> 00:00:48,575
For almost everything, a classical computer is better, and will stay better.

15
00:00:48,575 --> 00:00:54,012
A quantum computer is a specialised instrument, like a particle accelerator or a telescope.

16
00:00:54,012 --> 00:00:55,675
You don't put one in your pocket.

17
00:00:55,675 --> 00:00:58,087
You build one, and you point it at a problem.

18
00:00:58,087 --> 00:01:01,025
What it might do, and this is the part that matters.

19
00:01:01,025 --> 00:01:02,125
It might fail.

20
00:01:02,125 --> 00:01:06,375
Not because the physics is wrong, but because the engineering is too hard.

21
00:01:06,375 --> 00:01:09,937
The wiring, the cooling, the error rates, the cost.

22
00:01:09,937 --> 00:01:17,925
There is a real chance that in fifty years, quantum computing is remembered the way we remember fusion: always thirty years away.

23
00:01:17,925 --> 00:01:22,225
And what it will definitely do, whether or not it ever factors a single number.

24
00:01:22,225 --> 00:01:24,987
It will force us to answer the question Planck avoided.

25
00:01:24,987 --> 00:01:27,800
Because a quantum computer is not a metaphor.

26
00:01:27,800 --> 00:01:34,600
It is a physical object that behaves according to quantum mechanics, and produces results that a classical object cannot.

27
00:01:34,600 --> 00:01:41,975
If it works, if you can actually run a computation that no classical machine can follow, then the theory is not just useful.

28
00:01:41,975 --> 00:01:43,962
It is, in some sense, true.

29
00:01:43,962 --> 00:01:45,700
The superposition was real.

30
00:01:45,700 --> 00:01:47,325
The interference was real.

31
00:01:47,325 --> 00:01:51,975
The universe really does keep all those possibilities alive until you look.

32
00:01:51,975 --> 00:01:53,987
That is not a philosophical claim.

33
00:01:53,987 --> 00:02:02,712
It is an engineering claim, and it is being tested right now, in refrigerators the size of a person, at ten millikelvin, one cycle at a time.

34
00:02:02,712 --> 00:02:06,500
Planck spent his life trying to prove that the quanta were a mistake.

35
00:02:06,500 --> 00:02:07,762
He never succeeded.

36
00:02:07,762 --> 00:02:14,387
He died in nineteen forty-seven, a few years before the transistor, decades before the first quantum computer.

37
00:02:14,387 --> 00:02:16,600
He never saw what his trick would become.

38
00:02:16,600 --> 00:02:18,200
But he knew what it had cost him.

39
00:02:18,200 --> 00:02:21,637
In a letter written decades later, Planck admitted it plainly.

40
00:02:21,637 --> 00:02:31,000
My futile attempts to fit the elementary quantum of action into the classical theory continued for a number of years, and they cost me a great deal of effort.

41
00:02:31,000 --> 00:02:32,937
We are still crossing that bridge.

42
00:02:32,937 --> 00:02:36,200
The machine at the end of it is not a faster calculator.

43
00:02:36,200 --> 00:02:40,250
It is the first machine we have ever built that does not approximate nature.

44
00:02:40,250 --> 00:02:43,250
It borrows nature, and gives it back an answer.

45
00:02:43,250 --> 00:02:55,825
And if the answer is yes, if the universe really is made of quanta, and superposition, and probabilities that collapse only when someone looks, then the most beautiful idea in physics is not one we proved.

46
00:02:55,825 --> 00:02:58,025
It is one we are still learning to operate.

